to my hometown in Vermont, people are coming around to the idea that making room for the river is one of the best ways to prepare for extreme rainstorms. But whether Hamilton City can provide a workable blueprint for other small, working-class towns that desperately need protective infrastructure? I'm not so sure.
到我的家乡佛蒙特州，人们逐渐开始接受这样一种观点：为河流腾出空间是应对特大暴雨的最佳方法之一。但是，汉密尔顿城能否为其他迫切需要防护基础设施的小型工薪阶层城镇提供一个可行的蓝图？我不太确定。

77
00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:43.000
I mean, it's wonderful that you can point to an example where with a tremendous amount of perseverance and maybe a little bit of luck and outside assistance, we have a community that proved it could be done. Again, here's Rob Young. But, you know, I loved playing baseball and no matter how hard I work, I'm not going to be Shohei Otani. And so we, you know, we can't expect all of these communities to be able to pull it off in the same way.
我的意思是，你能指出这样一个例子，在付出巨大的毅力、或许还有一点点运气以及外界协助后，一个社区证明了这是可以做到的，这当然很棒。再次听听罗伯·扬（Rob Young）的说法。但是，你知道，我喜欢打棒球，无论我多么努力地训练，我都成不了大谷翔平。所以，你知道，我们不能指望所有这些社区都能以同样的方式办到这一点。

78
00:38:43.000 --> 00:39:02.000
For one thing, not every little town has a Jose and Leanne, the Shohei Otanis of Levy Building. What happened in Hamilton City is a cool story, but it doesn't change Rob Young's assessment of the overall problem. Any way you cut it, the deck is stacked against these small poor communities. And Rob's not naive.
首先，并不是每个小镇都有自己的何塞和莉安——筑堤界的大谷翔平。汉密尔顿城发生的事情是一个很酷的故事，但它并没有改变罗伯·扬对整体问题的评估。无论你从哪个角度来看，这些贫穷的小社区都处于劣势。罗伯并不是天真。

79
00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:43.000
He knows as well as anyone that we just won't be able to protect every community in the years ahead. That's just not possible. I mean, we have thousands of miles of coast. We have probably tens of thousands of miles of floodplain along rivers in the U.S. along rivers in the U.S. You know, the idea that we can guarantee protection to everybody, it's just not feasible. But if we can't build everyone a levy, we have to think long and hard about who is going to get the levies that we do build. Rob says that we need to have a national triage conversation
他比任何人都清楚，在未来的岁月里，我们根本无法保护每一个社区。这是绝对不可能的。我的意思是，我们拥有数千英里的海岸线。在美国的河流沿岸，可能还有数万英里的洪泛平原。你知道，那种我们可以保证每个人都受到保护的想法，根本是不切实际的。但是，如果我们不能为每个人都修建堤坝，我们就必须认真思考，究竟是谁能获得我们真正修建的堤坝。罗伯说，我们需要进行一场全国性的分诊式对话，

80
00:39:43.000 --> 00:40:12.000
about how we want to use our resources, about which communities should get priority and why. And again, it all comes down to what we value as a nation. These are our funds. These are public tax dollars. Certainly one would hope that the only calculus isn't going to be property value when we're talking about providing flood protection. In recent years, the Army Corps actually has started to rethink the benefit cost ratio.
讨论我们如何使用资源，哪些社区应该获得优先权以及为什么。归根结底，这一切都取决于我们作为一个国家所珍视的东西。这些是我们的资金。这是公共税收。人们当然希望，在讨论提供防洪保护时，唯一的计算标准不会仅仅是房产价值。近年来，陆军工程兵团实际上已经开始重新思考效益成本比。

81
00:40:12.000 --> 00:41:00.000
There is rulemaking in the works right now that could transform how the Corps makes decisions about who gets protection. You know, there are a wide variety of reasons why you might choose to invest those federal dollars beyond simply protecting high value real estate. But even if we do change the values that drive how these decisions are made, I think the Hamilton City story points to another massive problem as we try to adapt our infrastructure to climate change, which is speed. As disasters pile up all around the country, people need protection and they need it fast. And this whole process just takes so much time.
目前正在进行的规则制定工作可能会改变工程兵团在决定谁能获得保护时的决策方式。你知道，除了单纯保护高价值房地产之外，你选择投入这些联邦资金的原因可能还有很多。但是，即使我们改变了驱动这些决策的价值观，我认为汉密尔顿城的故事也指出了我们在试图使基础设施适应气候变化时的另一个巨大问题，那就是速度。随着灾难在全国各地不断堆积，人们需要保护，而且他们需要迅速得到保护。而整个过程却需要耗费如此多的时间。

82
00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:25.000
Here's Leanne Grigsby-Puente again. We've had people from out of the area ask us, how do you do this? And we always tell them, are you willing to put 25 years of your life in this to make sure it gets done? Because that's what we did. And that's what we tell them. I kind of couldn't believe it when I heard Leanne say that. Like, if I was one of those people trying to build a levy and I got that advice,
再次听听莉安·格里格斯比-蓬特（Leanne Grigsby-Puente）的话。我们有当地以外的人问我们：“你们是怎么做到的？”我们总是告诉他们：“你愿意把25年的生命投入到这上面来确保它完成吗？”因为这就是我们所做的事情。这就是我们对他们说的。当我听到莉安这么说时，我简直有些不敢相信。就好像如果我是那些试图修建堤坝的人之一，而我得到了这样的建议，

83
00:41:25.000 --> 00:41:46.000
I would give up right there on the spot. And as proud as Jose is of what they've accomplished, even he's not sure it was worth two decades of his life. I mean, if somebody was to ask me, hey, would you commit to this again? I don't know. On the one hand, I think this is a hopeful story of a small town overcoming the odds
我当场就会放弃。尽管何塞为他们所取得的成就感到自豪，但连他也不确定这是否值得他耗费二十年的光阴。我的意思是，如果有人问我：“嘿，你愿意再承诺做一次吗？”我不知道。一方面，我认为这是一个充满希望的故事，讲述了一个小镇克服重重困难，

84
00:41:46.000 --> 00:42:15.000
to get the kind of forward-thinking infrastructure it deserves. But on the other hand, it feels like they never should have had to work this hard. And the thing is, they're still not done. Ryan Luster says they need a couple more years to finish the habitat restoration. My goal is December of 2026. Probably is going to bleed into 2027. 27 years. Who's going to spend 27 years on a project?
获得了它应得的具有前瞻性的基础设施。但另一方面，感觉他们本不应该付出这么大的努力。问题是，他们还没有完工。瑞安·拉斯特（Ryan Luster）说，他们还需要几年时间来完成栖息地恢复工作。我的目标是2026年12月。可能会延续到2027年。27年。谁会花27年时间去做一个项目？

85
00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:53.000
I mean, I guess it's kind of a rhetorical question because I almost did. I guess just like when we're thinking about all the work that needs to be done to like adapt our infrastructure and adapt the way we're living to the challenges that are coming our way, it's you know, it's a hopeful story. And it's also a it's a sobering story. Exactly. It's a very sobering. Yeah, it's very sobering. But it's it shows that it's not impossible. that it's not impossible. This past winter, 2024, California got hit with more big storms. I wanted to see the new levee in action.
我的意思是，我想这算是个反问句，因为我几乎就这么做了。我想，就像当我们思考为了让基础设施适应环境、让我们的生活方式适应即将到来的挑战而需要做的大量工作时，你知道，这是一个充满希望的故事。同时这也是一个清醒的故事。没错，这是一个非常清醒的故事。是的，非常清醒。但它表明这并非不可能。这并非不可能。去年冬天，即2024年，加州遭遇了更多大风暴。我想看看新堤坝投入使用的实际效果。

86
00:42:53.000 --> 00:43:14.000
And so on a rainy weekend, I drove up to Hamilton City to meet with Jose. I said, we're on the Mazda today. OK, cool. Oh, yeah, the old truck. The old truck. It looks good. Yeah. It's an old beige pickup, one of those perfect tiny trucks they don't make anymore. Yeah, no, my baby's showing his age.
于是在一个下雨的周末，我开车去汉密尔顿城见何塞。我说，我们今天开马自达吗？好吧，酷。哦，对，老卡车。老卡车。它看起来真不错。是的。这是一辆旧米色皮卡，那种他们不再生产的完美小卡车。是的，不，我的宝贝显老了。

87
00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:36.000
How many miles? Oh, I got 300,000. 300,000? That's pretty good. Yeah. We take the old truck up onto the new levee. It honestly isn't much to look at, just a big wall of dirt. It's hard to believe how much time and money it took to get this built, but it makes all the difference in the world.
跑了多少英里了？哦，我开了30万英里。30万英里？那相当不错了。是的。我们开着老卡车上了新堤坝。老实说，它看起来没什么特别的，只是一堵巨大的泥墙。很难相信建造它花了这么时间和金钱，但它带来了天壤之别。

88
00:43:36.000 --> 00:44:26.000
On the other side is the floodplain, a giant space that's designed to fill right up with water, so that the town stays dry. Jose says there was a moment the night before when it was really raining quite hard. But instead of heading down to the levee with a bunch of sandbags, he went to bed. After many years, it's comfort to know that you can sleep better. Towards the end of our tour, we get to a spot where the river has flowed over the bank and onto the floodplain. It's not a lot of water, but it's enough that you can appreciate the way this landscape might have looked before, when this whole valley was a wetland.
另一边是洪泛平原，这是一个巨大的空间，设计成注满水，以便小镇保持干燥。何塞说，前天晚上雨下得非常大，当时有过那么一个时刻。但他没有带着一堆沙袋赶往堤坝，而是上床睡觉了。多年之后，这种能够睡个好觉的安稳感是一种慰藉。在参观快结束时，我们来到了一处河水漫过河岸流入洪泛平原的地方。水不是很多，但足以让你领略到这片土地以前的面貌，当时整个山谷都是湿地。

89
00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:39.000
Oh wow, look at all the birds. Yeah. Ducks. Yeah. They go where the water goes. See, this gives you a different perspective, doesn't it? Yeah. Totally.
哦，哇，看那些鸟。是的。鸭子。是的。水往哪里流，它们就去哪里。看，这给了你一个不同的视角，不是吗？是的。完全是这样。

90
00:44:39.000 --> 00:45:20.000
It's a beautiful scene. It almost makes you want to tear down all the levees and bring back the inland sea. Of course, that is a terrible idea. There are millions of people living in this valley, and the food grown here supports millions more. You can't let the rivers run wild. But you also can't just keep patching holes and crumbling old levees built right up against the riverbank and hope for the best. The Setback Levee at Hamilton City may not be a perfect model for every vulnerable river town, but I think it offers a hopeful vision of compromise.
这是一个美丽的场景。它甚至让你想拆掉所有的堤坝，把内陆海重新带回来。当然，那是个糟糕的主意。这个山谷里生活着数百万人，这里种植的粮食养活了更多的人。你不能让河流肆意泛滥。但你也不能总是修修补补那些紧邻河岸摇摇欲坠的老旧堤坝，然后祈祷一切顺利。汉密尔顿城的后退堤坝可能不是每个脆弱河滨小镇的完美模范，但我认为它提供了一个充满希望的妥协愿景。

91
00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:44.000
Proof that we can design for people and for nature at the same time. That we can choose to loosen our grip, relinquish some control, and give the river a little more room to breathe. When we got back to the lot where my car was parked, the winds had died down. The storm was just about over. Where are you headed now? Going home. Nice.
事实证明，我们可以同时为人类和自然进行设计。我们可以选择放松我们的掌控，放弃一部分控制权，给河流更多的呼吸空间。当我们回到我停车的停车场时，风已经停了。风暴差不多要结束了。你现在要去哪里？回家。太好了。

92
00:45:44.000 --> 00:46:22.000
Going home. Maybe take a nap now? Going home. Nice. Maybe take a nap. I don't know. You've you've earned it. All right. Next time on Not Built For This. So far this series has been all about the way climate change is impacting how and where we live. It's been about things like housing and infrastructure and insurance. But coming up on our final episode, we have a story about the impact of climate change
回家。现在也许可以睡个午觉？回家。太好了。也许睡个午觉。我不知道。你——你这是应得的。好的。下一期的《非此设计》。到目前为止，这个系列一直在探讨气候变化如何影响我们生活的方式和地点。它关乎住房、基础设施和保险之类的事情。但在即将播出的最后一集中，我们将讲述气候变化对我们的身体产生的影响。

93
00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:43.000
on our bodies. We're going to Phoenix, Arizona to see how extreme heat is revealing the biological limits of adaptation. Our thermometers top out at 109, and personally I've had multiple patients that have come in that have maxed out our thermometers. All we can do is try to find a shady spot to sit. Most times we're stuck out in the sun. It really sucks.
我们将前往亚利桑那州凤凰城，看看极端高温是如何显现出人类适应能力的生理极限的。我们的温度计最高显示到109华氏度（约43摄氏度），就我个人而言，我有好几个接诊的病人，他们的体温已经让我们的温度计爆表了。我们能做的就是找个阴凉的地方坐着。大多数时候我们都被困在太阳底下。这真的很糟糕。

